From 5910baf4b7a6d9550a3caef7f9e3c3ad3f64dbf3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:46:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/32] perf(qsa): one shared trtllm workspace + packed-KV
 scratch across QSA backends

Adaptive speculation builds one runtime state per candidate step count, and each
state constructs its own QSA backends: target verify, draft-extend, and
num_steps-1 draft decode steps. Every instance owned a private 128 MiB trtllm-gen
workspace and a private packed-KV gather buffer sized from its own verify window,
so the per-tier cost grew with tier depth. At candidate steps {1,3,5,7} that is
20 instances = 2560 MiB of workspace plus 234 MiB of gather buffers, and the deep
tiers ran the device out of memory while building the states.

Both regions are pure scratch with no state crossing a forward boundary: the
workspace is consumed inside one trtllm decode launch, and packed_k/packed_v are
fully rewritten (zero_fill_cols=stride, so the padding columns too) by the
extraction kernel immediately before the decode kernel reads them. One tier is
active at a time and the state swap happens between forwards, so one max-size set
serves all of them. This is the idiom the DSV4 and DSA backends already use for
the same kernel family via runtime_context.get_buffer; QSA was the outlier.

The gather buffer is planned before it is allocated: a captured graph bakes in its
address, so growing it after a capture would free the block an earlier tier
replays from. init_cuda_graph_state reserves at the DEEPEST reachable verify
window (max_speculative_num_draft_tokens), the first use allocates at the max of
every reservation, and a later reservation that would grow it raises. An eager
request above the plan (max_running_requests wider than any captured graph batch)
falls back to this backends private buffer, which no graph has captured.

Sharing is off under two-batch overlap and PDMux, the only paths that put a model
forward on a second stream, and behind SGLANG_QSA_SHARE_SCRATCH as an escape
hatch. The CUDA graph memory pool needed no change: every runner already resolves
its capture backend through resolve_decode_backend, which takes the one process
handle from get_or_create_global_graph_memory_pool. Routing is untouched, so the
duplicate-expert-id hazard in the fused MoE does not apply.

Recovered across the three extra tiers: 2219 MiB [arithmetic], about 87k bf16 KV
pool tokens at 26 KB/token.
